WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.765
This table is a computer.

00:00:03.265 --> 00:00:10.370
No electricity, no chips: just plywood, brass sliders and a circular slide rule.

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:16.239
It runs a neural network, the same algorithm that underpins modern AI.

00:00:17.239 --> 00:00:19.282
Start with a handwritten digit,

00:00:19.682 --> 00:00:21.958
shrunk to thirty-six squares.

00:00:22.758 --> 00:00:26.612
Each square corresponds to one slider on the outer ring:

00:00:27.113 --> 00:00:30.874
the more ink in the square, the further it slides.

00:00:32.074 --> 00:00:39.133
The next ring of sliders holds the weights: numbers the network learned from thousands of handwritten digits.

00:00:39.933 --> 00:00:41.651
They are everything it knows.

00:00:42.651 --> 00:00:44.137
And this is how it works.

00:00:44.637 --> 00:00:45.566
Take an input,

00:00:46.266 --> 00:00:47.520
take its weight,

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:50.681
and multiply them on the slide rule.

00:00:52.281 --> 00:00:55.114
Add the answer to a slider in the middle ring.

00:00:56.714 --> 00:00:58.247
Then do it again.

00:00:58.747 --> 00:01:01.069
Two hundred and sixteen times.

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:07.566
Anything that ends up below zero goes back to zero.

00:01:08.966 --> 00:01:13.610
Then once more: one more ring of weights, sixty more multiplications,

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:19.168
and the ten inner sliders hold the answer.

00:01:19.968 --> 00:01:22.243
The one that has travelled furthest wins.

00:01:23.143 --> 00:01:24.258
It's a seven.

00:01:26.458 --> 00:01:28.734
Those weights make it read digits.

00:01:29.334 --> 00:01:31.098
Slide them to new positions,

00:01:33.098 --> 00:01:35.745
and the same table does a different job.

00:01:36.745 --> 00:01:41.111
Now each input slider answers a yes-or-no question about an animal.

00:01:41.711 --> 00:01:42.918
Hair: yes.

00:01:43.318 --> 00:01:44.897
Feathers: no.

00:01:45.297 --> 00:01:46.319
Milk: yes.

00:01:47.519 --> 00:01:50.259
The same two hundred and seventy-six steps,

00:01:52.859 --> 00:01:54.623
and the output is mammal.

00:01:56.423 --> 00:01:57.817
Move the weights once more,

00:01:59.817 --> 00:02:01.767
and it guesses the next letter.

00:02:02.567 --> 00:02:04.425
After T, H:

00:02:07.025 --> 00:02:07.721
E.

00:02:09.521 --> 00:02:12.493
By hand, each answer takes the best part of an hour.

00:02:13.493 --> 00:02:18.462
A chip in a data centre does the same sums trillions of times a second.

00:02:19.662 --> 00:02:21.938
But nothing different is happening in there.

00:02:22.438 --> 00:02:25.457
It is multiplying and adding, all the way down.

00:02:26.657 --> 00:02:28.932
Here, you can watch every step.
